within GeneralRelativity;
package Cosmology "Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker cosmology: expansion history, recombination, acoustic waves and the CMB power spectrum"
  constant Real Gyr(unit = "s") = 1.0e9 * 365.25 * 86400.0 "Gigayear (Julian years)";
  constant Real MpcGyr = GeneralRelativity.Constants.Mpc / (GeneralRelativity.Constants.c * Gyr)
    "Light-travel time across 1 Mpc in Gyr; also 1 Mpc in Gly";
  annotation(Documentation(info="<html>
<p>Lambda-CDM cosmology on the FLRW metric <code>ds&sup2; = a&sup2;(&eta;)(&minus;d&eta;&sup2; + dx&sup2;)</code> (flat
by default), with the Planck 2018 parameters as defaults. Conformal quantities are in Mpc (with
<code>c = 1</code>: conformal time <code>&eta;</code> and comoving distances), cosmic time in Gyr.</p>
<h4>Expansion</h4>
<p><a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.hubble\">hubble</a> (the Friedmann equation),
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.cosmicTime\">cosmicTime</a>,
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.scaleFactorAtTime\">scaleFactorAtTime</a>,
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.conformalTime\">conformalTime</a> (the particle horizon),
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.comovingDistance\">comovingDistance</a>,
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.eventHorizon\">eventHorizon</a> and
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.soundHorizon\">soundHorizon</a>.
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Examples.CosmicExpansion\">Examples.CosmicExpansion</a> integrates the
Friedmann equation in time and writes the viewer's expanding-universe scene.</p>
<h4>Recombination and the CMB</h4>
<p><a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.thermalHistory\">thermalHistory</a> follows hydrogen
recombination (<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.peeblesRate\">Peebles' three-level atom</a>),
helium and reionization to the visibility function and the diffusion-damping scale.
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.acousticSources\">acousticSources</a> evolves linear
perturbations (cold dark matter, baryons, photons, neutrinos and the metric potentials; conformal Newtonian
gauge) for each wavenumber: the sound waves of the photon-baryon plasma.
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.cmbSpectrum\">cmbSpectrum</a> projects them onto the sky with
the line-of-sight integral and spherical Bessel functions to the temperature power spectrum
<code>D<sub>l</sub> = l(l+1)C<sub>l</sub>/2&pi;</code>, which
<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Examples.CosmicMicrowaveBackground\">Examples.CosmicMicrowaveBackground</a>
compares with the Planck 2018 measurements (<a href=\"modelica://GeneralRelativity.Cosmology.Data\">Data</a>).</p>
<p>This is a compact Boltzmann code in the spirit of Dodelson's <i>Modern Cosmology</i> and Ma &amp;
Bertschinger (ApJ 455, 7, 1995): photons and baryons are one tightly coupled fluid while Thomson scattering is
fast, then a photon Boltzmann hierarchy with polarization (so diffusion damping and the finite thickness of last
scattering come out of the equations), massless neutrinos with a truncated hierarchy, and the radiation-streaming
approximation late on. With the Planck 2018 parameters the temperature spectrum is within about 2.5% (rms,
<code>l = 30..1500</code>) of Planck's best-fit CAMB spectrum. Not modelled: CMB lensing (which smooths the peaks
by a few per cent at high <code>l</code>), the power regenerated by reionization at <code>l &lt; 20</code>, massive
neutrinos, polarization spectra, and helium recombination beyond Saha. It is not a replacement for CAMB or
CLASS.</p>
</html>"));
end Cosmology;
